> Quickly looking through the hadoop sources, it does do some sort of  
> reflection  - does that have any bearing?
>
>
> Yes. Usually Java code queries the class loader of the invoking  
> class (i.e. the loader by which the class was loaded). Therefore if  
> you use reflection in your code, it becomes crucial to have the  
> right class loader. First problem is if really bad code attempts to  
> use the bootstrap or system loader directly - that it completely  
> wrong and won't work (hopefully that's not the case here, but I  
> didn't check hadoop sources). Second problem arises when you set the  
> system loader to find some of your classes - e.g. via CLASSPATH. The  
> problem is that you make it possible for your classes to be loaded  
> by the system loader instead of the rJava loader. Consequently those  
> classes will use the wrong loader for reflection and thus won't find  
> classes. As long as you don't use reflection (and don't load further  
> packages that may use the same classes) everything may appear to  
> work, but it's going through the wrong loader.
>
> rJava uses its own loader because the Java system loader does not  
> allow changes to the class path. However, in R we need to  
> sequentially modify the class path as we load R packages using Java.  
> Unfortunately Java does not allow full replacement of class loaders,  
> it enforces a hierarchy that cannot be changed and the first two  
> loaders are always the bootstrap and system loader. Therefore it is  
> important to not let those loaders see any of your classes,  
> otherwise they will bypass the rJava loader. If you use rJava as  
> recommended, all is well, because the system and boot loaders will  
> have an empty class path, so all control is in rJava. However, when  
> you set CLASSPATH you will change that as described above.
>
> I hope this makes the potential issue clearer. Please do as I  
> recommended in the last e-mail (unset CLASSPATH and use classpath  
> parameter of .jinit) and let me know if the problem persists.
>
> Cheers,
> Simon
